\newlabel{tab:s1}{{4}{8}{$S{=}1$ falsification (peak stable throughput). The neighbor row gives the honest attribution for one-sided B-family traffic: a hot-inport \emph {lane-count} effect provided jointly by the pool budget and the escape exemption}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Pool activity vs.\ injection rate. Left: DP-CBS (A2) on two-sided transpose --- the pool is heavily exercised (\texttt  {dp\_shared\_grants}) and cap denials (\texttt  {dp\_pool\_blocks}) surge once the two directions start competing past saturation, confirming the counter works. On one-sided patterns the same counter is exactly zero: a denial requires the opposite side to hold budget. Right: DP-ESC (B4) on tornado --- \texttt  {dp\_pool\_blocks} is identically zero \emph  {by construction}, since pool pressure is absorbed by the route-time filter.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:activity}{{6}{8}{Pool activity vs.\ injection rate. Left: DP-CBS (A2) on two-sided transpose --- the pool is heavily exercised (\texttt {dp\_shared\_grants}) and cap denials (\texttt {dp\_pool\_blocks}) surge once the two directions start competing past saturation, confirming the counter works. On one-sided patterns the same counter is exactly zero: a denial requires the opposite side to hold budget. Right: DP-ESC (B4) on tornado --- \texttt {dp\_pool\_blocks} is identically zero \emph {by construction}, since pool pressure is absorbed by the route-time filter}{figure.6}{}}
